::JOUR_U01_L03_Q01::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which example is a media technology transition in journalism? {
~Changing a sentence from past tense to present tense#Incorrect. That is a wording revision, not a technology transition.
=Moving from printed monthly stories to same-day online updates#Correct. This changes the platform, timing, and audience access.
~Adding one adjective to a headline#Incorrect. This is a small writing change, not a media transition.
~Correcting a misspelled name#Incorrect. This is an accuracy correction, not a technology transition.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q02::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
How did radio change journalism technique compared with print? {
~It removed the need for clear wording.#Incorrect. Spoken news needs especially clear wording.
~It made all journalism visual.#Incorrect. Radio relies on sound and voice, not visuals.
=It made voice, sound, timing, and spoken delivery more important.#Correct. Radio changed how audiences received news.
~It prevented audiences from hearing updates.#Incorrect. Radio helped audiences hear updates.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q03::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
What is a likely audience change when news moves from television broadcast to website? {
=Audiences can choose links, revisit information, and receive updates outside one broadcast time.#Correct. Websites give audiences more control and access.
~Audiences can only watch at one scheduled time.#Incorrect. That describes traditional scheduled broadcast more than websites.
~Audiences lose all access to images.#Incorrect. Websites can include images and video.
~Audiences can no longer read text.#Incorrect. Websites often include text.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q04::MLA.JOUR.DIG.01
A strong digital presentation about media history should do what? {
~List random media tools with no connection.#Incorrect. Coherence requires clear connections.
~Use slides only for decoration.#Incorrect. Digital tools should support the idea.
~Claim that new technology makes accuracy unnecessary.#Incorrect. Accuracy remains necessary.
=Organize a clear explanation of how one technology changed journalism.#Correct. Coherent ideas and a clear perspective are required.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q05::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which statement best explains the shift from website article to mobile alert? {
~Mobile alerts are always longer than website articles.#Incorrect. Mobile alerts are usually shorter.
=Mobile alerts send brief information directly to an audience, so wording must be concise and accurate.#Correct. This explains both technique and audience effect.
~Mobile alerts remove the need for headlines.#Incorrect. Alerts often function like very short headlines.
~Mobile alerts make all context impossible forever.#Incorrect. Alerts can link to fuller context.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q06::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
What does the word focus mean in this lesson? {
=What a medium emphasizes, such as speed, depth, visuals, sound, or interaction#Correct. Focus describes what the platform or format highlights.
~The private opinion of the writer#Incorrect. Focus is about the medium's emphasis, not only opinion.
~The number of paragraphs in a notebook#Incorrect. Paragraph count is not the meaning here.
~The name of the printing company#Incorrect. That does not explain a journalism format's emphasis.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q07::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which trend is most connected to visual culture in digital journalism? {
~Removing all images from online stories#Incorrect. Digital visual culture often uses images and layout strategically.
~Ignoring how audiences view information on phones#Incorrect. Mobile habits are part of current visual culture.
=Using image cards, short captions, and mobile-friendly layouts#Correct. These design choices reflect digital audience habits.
~Publishing only plain paper handouts#Incorrect. This does not reflect digital media design trends.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q08::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Why is it incomplete to say online journalism is better simply because it is faster? {
~Speed is never part of online journalism.#Incorrect. Speed can be one important change.
~Online journalism cannot include facts.#Incorrect. Online journalism can and should include facts.
~Print journalism was never useful.#Incorrect. That claim is unsupported and inaccurate.
=Quality still depends on accuracy, clarity, audience needs, and format choices.#Correct. A complete explanation includes both change and responsibility.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q09::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which answer best explains a transition from print to television news? {
~Television uses only written columns and no images.#Incorrect. Television uses moving images and sound.
=Television adds moving images, sound, and scheduled broadcasts to journalism.#Correct. This explains a technology and technique change.
~Television removes the audience from the news process completely.#Incorrect. Audiences still receive and respond to news.
~Television makes verification impossible.#Incorrect. Verification remains possible and necessary.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q10::MLA.JOUR.DIG.01
Which digital presentation plan has the clearest perspective? {
=Show how mobile alerts changed speed and wording while accuracy stayed important.#Correct. This plan has a clear idea and focused explanation.
~Use ten images with no labels or explanation.#Incorrect. Visuals need organization and context.
~Say every platform is exactly the same.#Incorrect. The lesson focuses on meaningful differences.
~Avoid explaining the audience effect.#Incorrect. Audience effect is part of the standard focus.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q11::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
What should a student include when explaining a media-history transition? {
~Only the name of the newest platform#Incorrect. Naming the platform is not enough.
~Only a personal preference#Incorrect. Preference does not explain a transition.
=The older medium, newer medium, technique change, audience change, and quality concern#Correct. These parts create a complete explanation.
~A fictional story about technology#Incorrect. The task requires factual explanation.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q12::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which quality concern is especially important when publishing same-day web updates? {
~Making every update as long as a monthly feature#Incorrect. Same-day updates are often shorter.
~Using no headline at all#Incorrect. Headlines remain useful.
~Avoiding all audience awareness#Incorrect. Audience awareness matters.
=Verifying information quickly before posting#Correct. Speed increases the need for careful verification.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q13::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which comparison best identifies a technique change? {
=Print relies on page layout, while radio relies on spoken delivery and sound.#Correct. This compares how the journalism is presented.
~Both platforms should ignore facts.#Incorrect. Accuracy matters in both platforms.
~Print and radio are exactly the same in every way.#Incorrect. They use different techniques.
~Radio can never report news.#Incorrect. Radio is a major news medium.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q14::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
How can technology reflect trends in visual culture? {
~By preventing audiences from seeing any design#Incorrect. Visual culture involves how people see and understand design.
=By shaping layouts, images, icons, and mobile story cards that match audience habits#Correct. Design trends often follow technology and audience behavior.
~By making captions impossible#Incorrect. Captions remain important.
~By removing all platform differences#Incorrect. Platforms often create new design differences.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q15::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
What is the best explanation of a platform? {
~A source who gives a quote#Incorrect. A source provides information; a platform publishes it.
~A spelling rule for headlines#Incorrect. That is not a platform.
=A place or system where journalism is published or shared#Correct. Print, websites, apps, and social media are platforms.
~A private opinion paragraph#Incorrect. That describes content, not a platform.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q16::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which statement shows change and continuity? {
~New technology means accuracy is no longer required.#Incorrect. This ignores continuity.
~Old and new platforms are identical.#Incorrect. This ignores change.
~Audiences do not matter in digital journalism.#Incorrect. Audience needs still matter.
=Technology changes tools and techniques, but accuracy and clarity remain important.#Correct. This includes both change and continuity.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q17::MLA.JOUR.DIG.01
Which slide title best fits a coherent media-history presentation? {
=From Monthly Print to Same-Day Updates: How Speed Changed School Journalism#Correct. The title states a clear transition and perspective.
~Stuff About News#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~Pictures I Found Online#Incorrect. This does not show a coherent idea.
~Why Every Old Format Is Useless#Incorrect. This is unsupported and one-sided.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q18::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
What audience effect is most connected to social media posts? {
~Audiences must wait a month for all updates.#Incorrect. Social media can be immediate.
~Audiences cannot respond or share.#Incorrect. Social media often allows response and sharing.
=Audiences may receive, share, and respond to brief updates quickly.#Correct. Interaction and speed are important audience effects.
~Audiences can only listen to audio.#Incorrect. Social media can include text, images, audio, and video.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q19::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which explanation is too weak for this lesson? {
~Websites can update faster than monthly print, but they still need verification.#Incorrect. This is a strong explanation.
=New technology is cool.#Correct. This is too weak because it gives an opinion without explaining a journalism change.
~Television added moving images and sound to news presentation.#Incorrect. This explains a technique change.
~Mobile alerts require concise wording because space is limited.#Incorrect. This explains a format effect.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q20::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which design feature is most likely connected to phone-based news reading? {
~Tiny text that requires zooming on every line#Incorrect. Mobile design should be readable.
~A page layout that only works on large paper#Incorrect. That does not fit phone-based reading.
~Images with no captions or labels#Incorrect. Captions and labels help mobile audiences.
=Short headlines, clear image cards, and readable vertical layout#Correct. These choices reflect mobile visual culture.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q21::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
What is the best reason to study media history in journalism? {
=It helps journalists understand how tools shape format, audience, and responsibility.#Correct. Media history explains why journalism choices change.
~It proves only one platform should ever be used.#Incorrect. Different platforms serve different purposes.
~It removes the need to learn current formats.#Incorrect. History supports understanding current formats.
~It shows that audiences never change.#Incorrect. Audience habits do change.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q22::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which choice best explains technique in digital journalism? {
~The school subject a student likes most#Incorrect. That is unrelated to journalism technique.
~The private feeling a writer has about a topic#Incorrect. Technique is about method.
=The methods used to present information, such as links, video, updates, or interactive tools#Correct. These are digital presentation methods.
~The number of students in a classroom#Incorrect. That is not a journalism technique.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q23::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Why should a journalist avoid saying one platform is always best? {
~Because platforms never affect journalism#Incorrect. Platforms do affect journalism.
=Because each platform has strengths, limits, audience needs, and quality responsibilities#Correct. A balanced explanation considers purpose and context.
~Because old platforms are always better#Incorrect. That is also unsupported.
~Because new platforms cannot publish facts#Incorrect. New platforms can publish factual journalism.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q24::MLA.JOUR.DIG.01
What makes a digital presentation coherent? {
~Random slides with unrelated examples#Incorrect. Coherence requires connection.
~No clear beginning or conclusion#Incorrect. Organization matters.
~Decorations that hide the main idea#Incorrect. Design should support the message.
=Ideas are organized so each part supports the main perspective.#Correct. Coherent presentations connect evidence and explanation.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_Q25::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which response best shows Lesson 3 mastery? {
=Print to website changed publication speed and audience access, but journalists still need verification and clarity.#Correct. This explains transition, audience impact, and responsibility.
~Websites are newer, so they are automatically accurate.#Incorrect. Newer platforms still need verification.
~Print and websites have no differences.#Incorrect. They differ in speed, format, and audience interaction.
~Technology change only matters to computer programmers.#Incorrect. Technology change affects journalism choices.
}
